# Fernly API and end-to-end testing

## 1. Oracle

`docs/PRODUCT.md` and `/openapi.json` define what is right. When the app disagrees, keep the documented expectation and fail — never change the expectation to match the app; report it. What neither document says lives as a question in `FINDINGS.md` under "Questions": do not assert it until a human answers.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD — assert documented price-desc (most expensive first)
expect(items[0].price).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(items[1].price);

// ❌ BAD — loosen the expectation because the API returned something else
expect([items[0].price, items.at(-1)?.price]).toContain(maxPrice);
```

## 2. Black box

Never read or change `api/src/` or `web/src/`. Tests talk to HTTP and the browser only.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD
await request.get('/api/plants', { params: { sort: 'price-desc' } });

// ❌ BAD
import { store } from '../../api/src/store';
```

## 3. Requests

Use Playwright's `request` fixture with paths only. Host comes from `baseURL` (`API_URL`). Never hard-code a URL.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD
await request.get('/api/plants');

// ❌ BAD
await request.get('http://localhost:8100/api/plants');
```

## 4. Data

Every test creates what it needs. Never call `/api/admin/reset` in a test — the setup project does that once per run. Never depend on another test's data, except inside one serial flow.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD
const email = `shopper-${Date.now()}@fernly.test`;
await request.post('/api/auth/register', { data: { name: 'Pat', email, password: 'Fernly-Test-2026' } });

// ❌ BAD
await request.post('/api/admin/reset');
const email = 'ava@fernly.test'; // shared seed identity for mutating flows
```

## 5. Per endpoint

For each endpoint: positive (it works), negative (one invalid input, one change at a time → 400 `validation_error` or the business rule), errors (unknown id → 404).

```ts
// ✅ GOOD — GET /api/plants/{plantId} unknown id
test('unknown plant → 404', { tag: '@api' }, async ({ request }) => {
  expect((await request.get('/api/plants/pl-missing')).status()).toBe(404);
});

// ❌ BAD — only happy path for POST /api/cart/items, no negative or 404
```

## 6. Assertions

Check the status; for every 400, the exact `rule`; the values that matter, not only that a field exists; the shape with `expectSchema` from `tests/helpers/schema.ts`. A 400 with the wrong rule is a failed test.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD
expect(res.status()).toBe(400);
expect(body.rule).toBe('insufficient_stock');
await expectSchema(request, body, 'Error');

// ❌ BAD
expect(res.status()).toBe(400); // ignores wrong rule / no schema
expect(body).toHaveProperty('detail');
```

## 7. Tags

Exactly one tag per test: `{ tag: '@api' }` for one endpoint or `{ tag: '@e2e' }` for a flow. On `test()`, never on `describe()`.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD
test('GET /api/plants item matches Plant schema', { tag: '@api' }, async ({ request }) => { ... });
test('Price: high to low shows most expensive first', { tag: '@e2e' }, async ({ page }) => { ... });

// ❌ BAD
test.describe('plants', { tag: '@api' }, () => { ... });
test('flow', { tag: ['@api', '@e2e'] }, async () => { ... });
```

## 8. Known bugs

A test for a confirmed bug keeps its assertions and gets `test.fail(true, 'F-NN: <one line>')` — never deleted, never loosened.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD
test('price-desc returns most expensive first', { tag: '@api' }, async ({ request }) => {
  test.fail(true, 'F-02: price-desc sorts cheapest first');
  expect(items[0].price).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(items[1].price);
});

// ❌ BAD — delete the test or expect ascending order to “make CI green”
```

## 9. Evidence

Label every claim `executed`, `inspected only`, or `not yet tested`.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD
// executed — GET /api/health returns { status: "ok" }
// inspected only — seed customers listed in .env.example
// not yet tested — free shipping at exactly $50.00

// ❌ BAD — claim “all business rules covered” with no labels
```
